course: dictionary
lesson: 12224
title: One after another
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Consecutively means one after another, in order, with no gap or break. Think of the numbers 4, 5, 6: each one comes straight after the one before. The word describes how things follow each other.
explain: «The bus stopped at the school gate for three days consecutively.» That means Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday, with no day missed in between. If one day had been skipped, the days would not be consecutive. Check by swapping in «one after another» and seeing if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «The pages were numbered consecutively, from 1 to 50.» Nothing is missing and nothing is out of order. In sport, «Maya won four matches consecutively» means she won one match, then the next, then the next, with no loss in between.
fact: Consecutively means one after another, in order, without a gap or break.
fact: Consecutive numbers follow each other, such as 4, 5, 6.
fact: Useful phrases are «numbered consecutively» and «run consecutively».
fact: In formal writing, «consecutively» often means «in a row».
exercise: «The houses were numbered consecutively, 1, 2, 3 and so on.» What does consecutively mean here? | Very quickly and loudly | Mixed up at random | Painted in different colours | *In order, with none missing
exercise: Which set of numbers is consecutive? | 2, 5, 9 | 7, 8, 9 | 10, 30, 50 | *Never mind, that is wrong; the answer is 7, 8, 9
exercise: «The team lost three games consecutively.» What does this tell you? | They won three games | *They lost three games in a row, one after another | They lost three games, with wins in between | They played three games in different towns
test: «It rained for five days consecutively.» How many days in a row did it rain? | Five days, spread out across a month | Two days | *Five days, with no dry day between | Four days, with one dry day in the middle
test: Which list shows numbers in consecutive order? | 12, 14, 16 | *13, 14, 15 | 12, 13, 14 | 14, 12, 13
exam: Which sentence uses consecutively correctly? | *She read the chapters consecutively, from chapter one to chapter six | The shop is consecutively blue | She read consecutively the sad book under the table | The dog jumped consecutively over the moon and the cat
